Looking at this problem, I need to prove that `K.Total(s + t) == K.Total(s) + K.Total(t)` using induction on `|s|`.

Let me analyze the available lemmas:
- `End.Base(s, t)`: requires `|s| == 0`, ensures the goal
- `End.Finish(s, t)`: requires `|s| > 0` and `K.StepReady(s, t)`, ensures the goal
- `S1.Split(s, t)`: requires `|s| > 0`, ensures `K.SplitReady(s, t)`
- `S2.Lift(s, t)`: requires `|s| > 0`, `K.SplitReady(s, t)`, and `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)`, ensures `K.StepReady(s, t)`
- `Prove(s, t)`: recursive call with decreasing `|s|`

The proof strategy:
1. Base case: when `|s| == 0`, call `End.Base(s, t)`
2. Inductive case: when `|s| > 0`:
   - Call `Prove(s[1..], t)` to get `K.Total(s[1..] + t) == K.Total(s[1..]) + K.Total(t)` (inductive hypothesis)
   - Call `S1.Split(s, t)` to get `K.SplitReady(s, t)`
   - Call `S2.Lift(s, t)` to get `K.StepReady(s, t)`
   - Call `End.Finish(s, t)` to conclude

Let me verify the decreases clause: `Prove(s[1..], t)` has `|s[1..]| = |s| - 1 < |s|`, so it decreases properly.

